think Ran the ecrecover per-file witness GREEN: the algebraic round-trip (each of Zig's deterministic keys recovers back from its own (r,s) at exactly one recovery id and not the other, a one-bit hash tamper recovers a different key, the recovered key's Ethereum address matches through the GREEN eth_address.rye) AND our authored base-field square root equals Zig's std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1.fromSec1 byte-for-byte on R's recovered y across eight keys.
think Ran the count guard GREEN (38 files, bijection whole) and the whole crypto_suite_witness GREEN — all thirty-eight files rebuilt fresh from source and proven in dependency order, ecrecover registered after eth_address. The four doc edits (CONSTANT_TIME.md, README.md, docs/CRYPTO.md, suite prose) all synced to 38 files / 34 primitives.
obs Custody: recovery touches no secret — r, s, z, the recovery id, and the returned public key are all public by construction, so the one new arithmetic (a variable-time base-field square root a^((p+1)/4), valid because p ≡ 3 mod 4) leaks nothing. It answers who signed this?, never sign this; signing with the maintainer's own identity key stays the custody gate (#3/#4). This rung sits with verify on the non-gated side.
obs The secp256k1 tower now makes, checks, AND reads the sender out of a signature — the whole Ethereum account model's foundation (every Sign-in-with-Ethereum, every EIP-191/712 authentication, every transaction-sender derivation), authored in the open and checked against the world.
